#![TODO]
// TODO: closure escape leaks effects out of the type system.
//
// `inner` is a closure whose body invokes `println` (which requires
// `Stdout`), yet its declared type is `fn()` with no effects. Today the
// compiler accepts this without error and `pure_caller(f: fn())` ends up
// calling `println` even though it never declared `with Stdout`.
//
// Why it slips through:
//   * `effect_check.rs::TirExprKind::Closure` walks the body under the
//     enclosing function's `current_effects`, not under the closure's own
//     declared effects, so the `println` call passes (run() has Stdout).
//   * `resolver/closure.rs` synthesizes the closure type with empty
//     effects on purpose — adopting the contextual annotation's effects
//     would feed `EffectRef::Param` placeholders back into call-site
//     effect-param inference (see `effect_propagation_effect_param`).
//   * Indirect-call effect checks read the callee's *type* effects, which
//     are empty here, so `pure_caller` doesn't need to declare anything.
//
// To close this hole we need either:
//   (a) Body-effect inference at resolve time: walk the closure body and
//       attach the inferred effect set to the synthesized fn type, then
//       have the let / argument coercion paths reject widening from
//       a non-empty effect set to an empty one.
//   (b) effect_check swaps `current_effects` to the closure's declared
//       effects (propagation-expanded) when entering the body. Needs a
//       flag on `TirExprKind::Closure` to distinguish "explicitly empty
//       annotation" from "no annotation" so unannotated closures keep
//       inheriting outer effects.
//
// When fixed the call below should be rejected with an error mentioning
// `Stdout` (the leaked effect).

use { println, Stdout } from "core:cli";

fn pure_caller(f: fn()) {
    f();
}

export fn run() with Stdout {
    let inner: fn() = || {
        println("this println needs Stdout but inner's type does not carry it");
    };
    pure_caller(inner);
}

__DATA__
{"compile_error": "Stdout"}
